WEBVTT

00:01:12.460 --> 00:01:14.120
The year is 1958.

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:16.530
A thunderstorm was raging that night...

00:01:17.140 --> 00:01:19.790
The night the genius who descended 
into the darkness, Akagi Shigeru,

00:01:21.300 --> 00:01:23.500
the one who would come 
to be known as the man

00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:25.290
from the godly realm, 
saw his legend begin.

00:01:27.390 --> 00:01:30.860
Akagi had agreed to play for a man 
struggling with debt, Nangou.

00:01:30.860 --> 00:01:34.720
While playing against the yakuza, 
his talent begins to awaken.

00:01:36.700 --> 00:01:37.610
Riichi.

00:01:43.050 --> 00:01:45.120
<i>This guy... how could he discard</i>

00:01:45.890 --> 00:01:49.120
<i>that tile so calmly against that riichi?</i>

00:01:49.780 --> 00:01:52.120
<i>He's a complete novice, 
no matter how you look at it.</i>

00:01:52.580 --> 00:01:54.810
<i>Those guys don't get it, either.</i>

00:01:55.720 --> 00:01:57.720
<i>Somehow, for all his insane discards,</i>

00:01:57.720 --> 00:01:59.260
<i>he never deals into anyone's hand.</i>

00:01:59.960 --> 00:02:03.280
<i>I don't know if it's intuition or insight,</i>

00:02:03.790 --> 00:02:08.970
<i>but either way, I'm sure he was 
the kid from last night's incident!</i>

00:02:10.760 --> 00:02:14.610
<b>Episode 2</b>

00:02:10.760 --> 00:02:14.610
<b>The Awakening of Talent</b>

00:02:17.450 --> 00:02:20.080
It's South Hand 1. Ryuuzaki.

00:02:23.160 --> 00:02:23.960
Riichi.

00:02:31.390 --> 00:02:32.850
<i>Again?</i>

00:02:32.850 --> 00:02:35.070
<i>He's lucky no one called that...</i>

00:02:35.590 --> 00:02:39.140
<i>How can he keep discarding 
these dangerous tiles so easily?</i>

00:02:39.610 --> 00:02:44.080
<i>Is this the luck he was born with?</i>

00:02:48.260 --> 00:02:52.950
<i>With this hand, you'd probably 
want to discard the 5 of bamboo...</i>

00:02:53.470 --> 00:02:56.590
<i>But it's unwise, since it's clear from 
the discards that it's an ura suji.</i>

00:02:56.590 --> 00:02:59.590
<i>On top of that, it's a dora. 
You simply can't discard that.</i>

00:03:00.710 --> 00:03:03.230
<i>Since he can see where all four 
of the 3 of characters tiles are,</i>

00:03:03.230 --> 00:03:05.070
<i>he should discard the 2 of characters...</i>

00:03:07.550 --> 00:03:09.540
H-Hang on a sec!

00:03:11.170 --> 00:03:12.910
I know asking you to read the tiles in

00:03:12.910 --> 00:03:14.390
the discard pile is too much to ask for,

00:03:14.390 --> 00:03:15.910
but that's just too crazy.

00:03:17.290 --> 00:03:19.230
Is this tile that dangerous?

00:03:20.030 --> 00:03:22.810
That's Ryuuzaki's ura suji.

00:03:23.430 --> 00:03:27.110
Ura suji is, for instance, getting a 
3 of bamboo with a hand like this.

00:03:27.110 --> 00:03:28.800
The 6 of bamboo will be discarded.

00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:31.830
In Mahjong, this mechanism 
makes it very likely that

00:03:31.830 --> 00:03:33.560
the wait will be for a 2 or 5 of bamboo.

00:03:34.170 --> 00:03:36.530
On top of that, the 5 of bamboo is a dora.

00:03:36.530 --> 00:03:37.240
Dora?

00:03:37.520 --> 00:03:38.920
A dora is a handy tile

00:03:38.920 --> 00:03:41.570
that gives you an additional 
han just for having it.

00:03:43.900 --> 00:03:45.440
I didn't know that.

00:03:46.220 --> 00:03:48.570
But that doesn't matter.

00:03:49.300 --> 00:03:50.150
Hey!

00:03:53.170 --> 00:03:55.850
See, this one went through.

00:03:56.300 --> 00:03:57.590
You idiot!

00:03:57.980 --> 00:04:00.850
If you keep playing like that, you'll eventually...

00:04:03.790 --> 00:04:04.550
Ron.

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:07.990
What?!

00:04:07.990 --> 00:04:09.730
Waiting for a 2 or 5 of characters?!

00:04:10.670 --> 00:04:11.760
Don't tell me...

00:04:12.800 --> 00:04:13.730
I knew it.

00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:16.470
I figured this area was 
the most problematic.

00:04:17.680 --> 00:04:19.070
Why?

00:04:19.070 --> 00:04:20.950
How did you know that?

00:04:20.950 --> 00:04:22.370
I heard the voices.

00:04:22.370 --> 00:04:23.070
What?

00:04:24.600 --> 00:04:26.660
Everybody's distracted by the noise

00:04:26.660 --> 00:04:28.580
and simply can't hear their voices.

00:04:29.650 --> 00:04:31.130
But I can hear them.

00:04:31.550 --> 00:04:33.130
The voices of the three groups of discards.

00:04:37.490 --> 00:04:42.070
In the end, Ryuuzaki loses 
first place again this hand.

00:04:43.110 --> 00:04:46.090
After the sixth game, 
Nangou is at +49.

00:04:46.470 --> 00:04:48.430
Only two games remain.

00:04:49.420 --> 00:04:50.780
This is ridiculous.

00:04:51.270 --> 00:04:54.440
We're getting beaten by that amateur kid.

00:04:54.910 --> 00:04:58.310
At this rate, I'm going to have to shoulder

00:04:59.220 --> 00:05:00.970
that scumbag's three million yen debt.

00:05:02.590 --> 00:05:06.080
Call Yagi. Call our rep player, Yagi!

00:05:06.380 --> 00:05:07.080
Yes, sir!

00:05:08.610 --> 00:05:12.090
None of this makes any sense.

00:05:12.440 --> 00:05:15.170
You only started playing 
Mahjong a few hours ago,

00:05:15.170 --> 00:05:18.290
yet not only have you not dealt 
into Ryuuzaki's hand once,

00:05:18.290 --> 00:05:20.100
you also came out on top.

00:05:21.020 --> 00:05:23.000
Can you tell us...

00:05:23.340 --> 00:05:26.000
What you're seeing right now?

00:05:30.110 --> 00:05:31.010
Detective,

00:05:31.760 --> 00:05:35.010
what would you discard from this hand?

00:05:36.110 --> 00:05:38.140
The south or west wind, I'd say.

00:05:39.740 --> 00:05:42.910
Then, let's say those honor 
tiles have been taken care of.

00:05:42.910 --> 00:05:44.020
How about this hand?

00:05:45.150 --> 00:05:46.650
8 of characters.

00:05:47.330 --> 00:05:48.560
And from this one?

00:05:49.380 --> 00:05:50.560
7 of circles!

00:05:51.250 --> 00:05:52.630
7 of circles...

00:05:53.660 --> 00:05:56.310
Detective, do you see the difference

00:05:56.310 --> 00:05:58.640
between this 7 of circles 
and the 8 of characters?

00:05:59.150 --> 00:05:59.920
Difference?

00:05:59.920 --> 00:06:00.640
What?

00:06:01.590 --> 00:06:04.820
At first glance, it appears the 7 of circles and 
8 of characters were discarded similarly.

00:06:04.820 --> 00:06:07.050
However, they hold completely 
opposite discard information.

00:06:08.530 --> 00:06:11.130
When the 8 of characters was discarded, 
it wasn't connected to

00:06:11.130 --> 00:06:12.500
any wait in our hand.

00:06:13.170 --> 00:06:15.330
On the other hand, 
this 7 of circles is connected to

00:06:15.330 --> 00:06:17.300
a wait and is therefore a dangerous tile.

00:06:18.130 --> 00:06:20.860
If you were to classify discards in Mahjong,

00:06:20.860 --> 00:06:22.040
there are essentially two groups.

00:06:22.540 --> 00:06:25.150
Those in the safe zone, 
and those in the danger zone.

00:06:25.800 --> 00:06:27.310
Of course, there are tiles that don't

00:06:27.310 --> 00:06:30.830
clearly fit into either group and 
aren't much more than noise.

00:06:30.830 --> 00:06:32.510
If you include that, 
you have three groups.

00:06:33.200 --> 00:06:35.210
Every discarded tile can be 100% explained

00:06:35.210 --> 00:06:37.170
with these three groups.

00:06:38.870 --> 00:06:40.680
Do you remember these discarded tiles?

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:45.920
Those are Ryuuzaki's tiles 
when you discarded the dora.

00:06:46.420 --> 00:06:49.550
Right. Nangou-san tried to stop me,

00:06:49.550 --> 00:06:51.970
but to me, it was clear that it would pass.

00:06:53.440 --> 00:06:55.230
He discarded the 6 of bamboo too early.

00:06:55.690 --> 00:06:58.840
That means the 6 of bamboo's worth was lower

00:06:58.840 --> 00:07:00.900
than the 9 of circles and 8 of 
characters that came after,

00:07:00.900 --> 00:07:02.960
meaning he had none 
of the surrounding tiles.

00:07:03.410 --> 00:07:06.850
Even if he did, they would've been 
closer to the 2 or 3 of bamboo.

00:07:06.850 --> 00:07:08.300
But that possibility also disappeared

00:07:08.300 --> 00:07:10.570
when he discarded the 1 of bamboo 
after drawing it in the eighth turn.

00:07:11.380 --> 00:07:12.940
He had no bamboo tiles left.

00:07:13.790 --> 00:07:15.810
From the early discard of the 6 of bamboo,

00:07:15.810 --> 00:07:18.450
it was basically clear that he had 
no bamboo tiles left in his hand.

00:07:19.070 --> 00:07:21.090
The area around the 
6 of bamboo is a safe zone.

00:07:21.560 --> 00:07:23.720
But the area around the 1 of 
characters that he'd clung to

00:07:23.720 --> 00:07:24.960
until the sixth turn...

00:07:25.940 --> 00:07:28.960
combined with the discarded 
4 of characters afterward,

00:07:28.960 --> 00:07:31.960
made it pretty obvious that he was 
waiting for a 2 or 5 of characters.

00:07:32.590 --> 00:07:34.160
Meaning that this 1 of characters

00:07:34.160 --> 00:07:35.770
was the classic example of the danger zone.

00:07:38.830 --> 00:07:41.970
This is how I classify my opponent's discards.

00:07:42.960 --> 00:07:45.140
And the tile that didn't fit in either category...

00:07:45.960 --> 00:07:48.480
This is the noise I mentioned earlier.

00:07:49.100 --> 00:07:50.350
Noise?

00:07:50.350 --> 00:07:51.560
Noise, huh?

00:07:51.990 --> 00:07:54.240
Ryuuzaki has a bit of a habit.

00:07:54.830 --> 00:07:55.720
A habit?

00:07:57.000 --> 00:07:58.280
That time, Ryuuzaki...

00:08:00.170 --> 00:08:00.860
Riichi.

00:08:01.950 --> 00:08:04.860
After declaring riichi, 
he looked at his tiles,

00:08:04.860 --> 00:08:07.190
then glanced to the left end of his hand.

00:08:08.020 --> 00:08:09.430
The left end...

00:08:09.860 --> 00:08:12.840
Yes. That means he 
looked at his character tiles.

00:08:13.970 --> 00:08:16.310
He was double-checking 
that he was in tenpai.

00:08:17.230 --> 00:08:20.130
From that moment, the chance of him 
waiting for circles was also gone.

00:08:20.130 --> 00:08:23.350
He was only waiting for 
character tiles, namely 2 or 5.

00:08:25.150 --> 00:08:26.230
<i>He's a monster!</i>

00:08:26.580 --> 00:08:28.850
<i>He only started playing 
Mahjong a few hours ago</i>

00:08:28.850 --> 00:08:31.090
<i>and has already uncovered the 
essence of the discarded tiles!</i>

00:08:31.490 --> 00:08:34.040
<i>And his insight is frightening...</i>

00:08:34.040 --> 00:08:36.010
<i>Ryuuzaki is no match for him.</i>

00:08:36.010 --> 00:08:37.780
<i>He's already surpassed him by far.</i>

00:08:38.840 --> 00:08:43.190
<i>The talent of a genius has awakened in him.</i>

00:08:46.930 --> 00:08:48.450
The showdown resumes.

00:08:48.450 --> 00:08:52.240
It's the seventh game, the third 
East-South match since Akagi joined.

00:08:54.010 --> 00:08:55.580
<i>I'm counting on you, Akagi.</i>

00:08:56.430 --> 00:08:59.940
<i>Stay on this wave and finish it!</i>

00:09:02.260 --> 00:09:04.320
It's as if Nangou's prayer worked.

00:09:04.940 --> 00:09:07.220
Akagi's doing well again 
in this East-South match.

00:09:07.650 --> 00:09:08.760
After the East round,

00:09:08.760 --> 00:09:10.410
Akagi is in the lead, 22,000 points ahead

00:09:10.410 --> 00:09:11.950
of the second place Ryuuzaki.

00:09:12.680 --> 00:09:15.010
It seems as if this East-South 
match is already decided.

00:09:15.390 --> 00:09:16.830
But then...

00:09:23.320 --> 00:09:27.580
<b>Yagi Keiji</b>

00:09:24.300 --> 00:09:27.180
The rep player, Yagi Keiji, arrives.

00:09:27.930 --> 00:09:30.310
Yagi! I've been waiting for you!

00:09:31.910 --> 00:09:34.690
Ryuuzaki-san, I hear you're 
having a tough time.

00:09:35.490 --> 00:09:40.100
I've seen the kid's tiles, 
so I can't play this hand.

00:09:40.780 --> 00:09:43.170
Shall I start in the next hand, then?

00:09:43.170 --> 00:09:44.440
Could you?

00:09:44.440 --> 00:09:47.460
Don't worry, we still have 
the entire South round to go.

00:09:47.920 --> 00:09:50.300
I'll win this East-South match for you.

00:09:51.190 --> 00:09:54.910
At that moment, Yagi didn't even 
consider Akagi to be an opponent.

00:09:55.500 --> 00:09:57.130
That's only to be expected.

00:09:57.630 --> 00:10:00.480
After all, Akagi is a middle school student.

00:10:02.940 --> 00:10:07.490
However, his opinion would quickly change.

00:10:09.320 --> 00:10:10.690
The match resumes.

00:10:10.690 --> 00:10:12.320
The dealer's first discard...

00:10:13.360 --> 00:10:14.060
Pon.

00:10:14.840 --> 00:10:16.330
Ryuuzaki calls once.

00:10:16.330 --> 00:10:17.320
Is it a rush?

00:10:19.000 --> 00:10:21.250
And now, Akagi's first draw.

00:10:22.130 --> 00:10:23.570
A pair of red dragons.

00:10:24.030 --> 00:10:25.300
<i>He's lucky.</i>

00:10:25.810 --> 00:10:28.510
<i>Since he's in the lead, 
an express ticket to early victory,</i>

00:10:28.510 --> 00:10:31.380
<i>like the red dragon pair, 
is just what he needs.</i>

00:10:32.380 --> 00:10:35.180
However, Akagi had a certain hunch...

00:10:36.280 --> 00:10:36.950
What?

00:10:38.340 --> 00:10:40.020
The next turn, he discards another red dragon!

00:10:40.520 --> 00:10:43.570
Yasuoka can't believe that he 
discarded the red dragon pair.

00:10:43.570 --> 00:10:45.290
At that moment, Ryuuzaki thinks...

00:10:48.150 --> 00:10:50.530
<i>Keeping the red dragon won't do any good.</i>

00:10:52.120 --> 00:10:53.800
But in the next turn...

00:10:56.090 --> 00:10:57.620
<i>Damn it!</i>

00:10:59.320 --> 00:11:01.170
And the turn after that...

00:11:03.760 --> 00:11:04.910
<i>Crap!</i>

00:11:05.780 --> 00:11:07.910
<i>If I hadn't discarded the red dragon...</i>

00:11:13.230 --> 00:11:13.850
Pon!

00:11:15.560 --> 00:11:17.950
Akagi's reason for discarding 
his pair of red dragons

00:11:17.950 --> 00:11:19.460
finally becomes apparent.

00:11:20.020 --> 00:11:21.280
<i>He has such a keen sense that</i>

00:11:21.280 --> 00:11:24.080
<i>he smelled the daisangen just 
from the white dragon being called.</i>

00:11:24.080 --> 00:11:25.570
<i>He foresaw the danger.</i>

00:11:26.340 --> 00:11:28.760
<i>The strange move of dropping 
the two red dragons</i>

00:11:28.760 --> 00:11:31.470
<i>killed off Ryuuzaki's potential daisangen.</i>

00:11:32.720 --> 00:11:33.930
A few turns later,

00:11:33.930 --> 00:11:36.470
Akagi is one tile away from 
completing a sanshoku.

00:11:37.010 --> 00:11:39.710
But, seeing that Ryuuzaki 
can win any time...

00:11:40.160 --> 00:11:40.940
Chi.

00:11:42.820 --> 00:11:45.320
Without any remorse, he throws 
away the sanshoku...

00:11:46.420 --> 00:11:47.410
Ron.

00:11:49.380 --> 00:11:50.820
...and takes a 1,000 point win.

00:11:54.230 --> 00:11:57.390
<i>He moves quickly. 
His insight is spot on.</i>

00:11:57.390 --> 00:12:00.330
<i>A normal player would be 
hesitant to drop the sanshoku,</i>

00:12:00.780 --> 00:12:05.880
<i>but this kid knows how much that 
hesitation would slow him down.</i>

00:12:06.840 --> 00:12:08.490
<i>His sense is miles ahead...</i>

00:12:08.490 --> 00:12:11.000
<i>Ryuuzaki is simply no match for him.</i>

00:12:11.510 --> 00:12:17.070
<i>It's like a shootout between a scoped 
rifle and a crudely modded gun.</i>

00:12:17.070 --> 00:12:19.050
<i>He'll be shot down 
without having a chance.</i>

00:12:19.640 --> 00:12:24.510
<i>The only concern now is this man, Yagi Keiji.</i>

00:12:29.100 --> 00:12:31.620
You want to keep observing him 
the rest of this East-South match?

00:12:31.620 --> 00:12:35.980
Yes. We're 23,000 points behind, 
and it's already South Hand 2.

00:12:37.010 --> 00:12:40.960
To turn it around from here, 
I'd have to overextend quite a bit.

00:12:40.960 --> 00:12:43.770
And that kid's actually quite formidable.

00:12:44.200 --> 00:12:47.000
I heard he's a beginner.

00:12:47.000 --> 00:12:50.280
That makes him even more eerie 
as an unknown quantity.

00:12:51.230 --> 00:12:52.890
It's best to not underestimate him.

00:12:53.320 --> 00:12:55.350
Observing him for the rest of this 
East-South match is safest.

00:12:57.410 --> 00:12:59.760
The match resumes with South Hand 2.

00:13:01.690 --> 00:13:05.050
<i>I see. This guy isn't stupid.</i>

00:13:05.050 --> 00:13:07.830
<i>Did he pick up on Akagi's 
talent with one glance?</i>

00:13:08.200 --> 00:13:09.940
<i>The opponent's only a middle schooler.</i>

00:13:10.360 --> 00:13:12.500
<i>Most people would want to show 
off in a situation like this.</i>

00:13:12.500 --> 00:13:14.590
<i>His restraint is admirable.</i>

00:13:14.590 --> 00:13:18.010
<i>He's definitely a higher 
ranked player than Ryuuzaki.</i>

00:13:21.210 --> 00:13:22.760
Akagi's one tile away from tenpai.

00:13:23.260 --> 00:13:25.150
It's already the eighth turn.

00:13:30.580 --> 00:13:31.420
What?!

00:13:33.380 --> 00:13:35.720
At that moment, the player before Akagi

00:13:35.720 --> 00:13:37.880
was in tenpai with a 
junchan sanshoku hand,

00:13:35.720 --> 00:13:37.880
Junchan (jun chanta) - A "pure" chanta with 
terminals in each set, and no honor tiles.

00:13:37.880 --> 00:13:39.300
with a closed wait for an 8 of bamboo.

00:13:37.880 --> 00:13:39.300
Closed wait - Waiting for the middle tile in a sequence.

00:13:41.200 --> 00:13:43.510
<i>How did he avoid discarding the 8 of bamboo?</i>

00:13:44.090 --> 00:13:46.130
<i>From the previous player's discards,</i>

00:13:46.130 --> 00:13:49.440
<i>it would've made sense to discard his 
least useful tile, the 8 of bamboo.</i>

00:13:50.300 --> 00:13:52.950
<i>He sensed that the 8 of 
bamboo is dangerous and</i>

00:13:52.950 --> 00:13:54.440
<i>discarded the 5 of bamboo instead...</i>

00:13:55.920 --> 00:13:58.340
<i>What can this kid see?</i>

00:13:59.070 --> 00:14:00.320
The following turn.

00:14:03.520 --> 00:14:04.910
<i>I can't believe what he just drew.</i>

00:14:05.620 --> 00:14:07.690
<i>Not only does he seem to see 
through everybody's hands,</i>

00:14:07.690 --> 00:14:09.070
<i>he also has incredible luck.</i>

00:14:09.070 --> 00:14:10.460
<i>An 8 of bamboo triplet...</i>

00:14:13.830 --> 00:14:17.770
<i>He basically holds all the winning 
tiles of the player before him.</i>

00:14:18.280 --> 00:14:19.500
The turn after that.

00:14:24.630 --> 00:14:25.510
Riichi.

00:14:27.230 --> 00:14:29.790
Akagi declares riichi with a wait 
for the 2 or 5 of characters.

00:14:30.380 --> 00:14:33.490
The detective Yasuoka, watching from 
behind him, doesn't notice anything.

00:14:33.880 --> 00:14:35.620
Obviously, neither does Nangou.

00:14:35.920 --> 00:14:36.820
However,

00:14:37.610 --> 00:14:39.270
a shock runs through Yagi.

00:14:40.330 --> 00:14:42.350
<i>Riichi after discarding the 6 of circles...</i>

00:14:42.810 --> 00:14:44.010
<i>That was a mistake.</i>

00:14:44.670 --> 00:14:47.880
<i>If he had to declare riichi from this hand, 
he should have discarded the 5 of circles.</i>

00:14:48.500 --> 00:14:49.630
<i>If he drew a 4 of circles,</i>

00:14:48.500 --> 00:14:51.570
Kan - Four of a kind that is revealed to the other players.

00:14:49.630 --> 00:14:51.570
<i>he could have declared kan, as well.</i>

00:14:51.570 --> 00:14:53.430
<i>Since that would give him 
a draw from the dead wall</i>

00:14:53.430 --> 00:14:55.270
<i>and an additional dora indicator,</i>

00:14:55.270 --> 00:14:57.540
<i>there was no choice but 
to discard the 5 of circles.</i>

00:14:57.540 --> 00:14:59.560
<i>In other words, his riichi 
should have looked like this.</i>

00:15:00.590 --> 00:15:02.560
<i>Why didn't he do it that way?</i>

00:15:04.150 --> 00:15:07.760
<i>I bet he thought that he couldn't 
declare kan after declaring riichi.</i>

00:15:07.760 --> 00:15:09.290
<i>He still is just a beginner.</i>

00:15:10.320 --> 00:15:11.260
<i>I can beat him.</i>

00:15:11.600 --> 00:15:14.520
<i>I'll take advantage of that 
opening and destroy his game.</i>

00:15:15.230 --> 00:15:19.160
<i>The smarter they are, the worse 
they stumble once thrown off balance.</i>

00:15:21.530 --> 00:15:24.940
In the end, Akagi dominated 
the seventh game.

00:15:25.730 --> 00:15:28.470
If Akagi wins one more game,

00:15:28.470 --> 00:15:31.920
all of Nangou's debts will be paid off, 
and it will all be over.

00:15:33.170 --> 00:15:34.950
Can you win?

00:15:34.950 --> 00:15:37.180
Just trust me.

00:15:37.910 --> 00:15:38.930
Except...

00:15:40.670 --> 00:15:43.090
I have a request.

00:15:44.760 --> 00:15:48.230
Don't you think this is an unfair fight?

00:15:49.180 --> 00:15:50.230
Unfair?

00:15:50.530 --> 00:15:52.940
Yeah. Think about it.

00:15:52.940 --> 00:15:54.810
Ryuuzaki-san, your opponent up to now,

00:15:54.810 --> 00:15:56.690
will lose a tremendous amount 
of money if he loses.

00:15:57.070 --> 00:15:58.810
And, as his rep player,

00:15:58.810 --> 00:16:03.180
losing to an amateur will mean 
that my career will be over.

00:16:03.180 --> 00:16:04.740
But what about you?

00:16:05.470 --> 00:16:09.750
If you lose, only Nangou will be in trouble.

00:16:10.370 --> 00:16:12.490
You won't lose anything.

00:16:13.190 --> 00:16:14.250
What are you getting at?

00:16:15.720 --> 00:16:18.620
It's quite simple. 
Let's make a personal wager.

00:16:19.170 --> 00:16:20.990
100,000 yen on one East-South match.

00:16:22.130 --> 00:16:26.450
I realize that you can't get 
that kind of money right now.

00:16:26.450 --> 00:16:31.090
That's why I'm willing to 
accept a substitute for it.

00:16:31.090 --> 00:16:32.240
What kind of substitute?

00:16:33.450 --> 00:16:36.740
One finger for 100,000 yen.

00:16:36.740 --> 00:16:38.470
N-Now just a minute...

00:16:39.090 --> 00:16:39.900
Sure.

00:16:41.350 --> 00:16:42.550
If it's the same.

00:16:42.820 --> 00:16:43.680
The same?

00:16:44.000 --> 00:16:48.690
Yeah. You don't look like you 
have that kind of money, either.

00:16:49.710 --> 00:16:53.170
If you're willing to equally pay 
with a finger when you lose,

00:16:53.170 --> 00:16:54.540
I don't mind.

00:16:54.540 --> 00:16:55.820
<i>You little shit...</i>

00:16:56.730 --> 00:16:58.470
Of course, I accept.

00:16:58.470 --> 00:17:00.610
Don't forget what you just said!

00:17:02.570 --> 00:17:04.540
<i>He didn't see that coming.</i>

00:17:04.540 --> 00:17:05.830
<i>That was just a bluff.</i>

00:17:05.830 --> 00:17:10.270
<i>He tried to scare Akagi and 
overwhelm him with pressure.</i>

00:17:10.820 --> 00:17:13.810
<i>This kid parried that elegantly.</i>

00:17:14.830 --> 00:17:16.310
<i>He's got guts.</i>

00:17:16.310 --> 00:17:19.340
<b>- Eighth Game -</b>

00:17:18.270 --> 00:17:21.350
When gambling, having something to lose

00:17:21.350 --> 00:17:23.590
changes the game fundamentally.

00:17:24.280 --> 00:17:26.110
When there's nothing to lose,

00:17:26.110 --> 00:17:29.220
one's intuition and concentration 
are the deciding factors.

00:17:29.660 --> 00:17:32.060
But when there is something at stake,

00:17:32.060 --> 00:17:35.070
those abilities alone will 
not carry you to victory.

00:17:35.070 --> 00:17:37.320
It doesn't matter how well you 
can read your opponent's hands

00:17:37.320 --> 00:17:40.070
if you can't trust your own conclusion.

00:17:40.850 --> 00:17:42.050
If you lack the capacity

00:17:42.050 --> 00:17:45.510
to entrust your fate to your 
conclusion, it's all meaningless.

00:17:46.510 --> 00:17:49.750
Which means your will to trust yourself,

00:17:49.750 --> 00:17:51.720
your soul, is what is being tested.

00:17:53.030 --> 00:17:53.990
Riichi.

00:17:59.080 --> 00:18:00.910
Yagi is in a closed wait for a 7 of bamboo.

00:18:02.550 --> 00:18:05.830
<i>Yagi is already bluffing.</i>

00:18:05.830 --> 00:18:07.640
<i>With a hand like this, you'd usually wait</i>

00:18:07.640 --> 00:18:09.450
<i>for a two-sided wait before declaring riichi.</i>

00:18:09.450 --> 00:18:11.070
<i>But he went for it without hesitation.</i>

00:18:11.310 --> 00:18:13.640
<i>A preemptive breakaway riichi.</i>

00:18:13.640 --> 00:18:16.070
<i>Yagi wants to see how Akagi will react.</i>

00:18:16.430 --> 00:18:18.570
<i>The nature of the game has changed.</i>

00:18:20.650 --> 00:18:22.310
Now Akagi is in tenpai, as well.

00:18:22.830 --> 00:18:24.570
<i>This is interesting.</i>

00:18:24.570 --> 00:18:26.050
<i>If Akagi hasn't wavered,</i>

00:18:26.050 --> 00:18:28.280
<i>he'll determine that the 
7 of characters discarded</i>

00:18:28.280 --> 00:18:30.870
<i>in the second turn is a safe zone, 
and discard his 6 of characters.</i>

00:18:31.540 --> 00:18:35.160
<i>But if there is any doubt in his heart...</i>

00:18:35.160 --> 00:18:37.060
But Akagi doesn't hesitate.

00:18:39.410 --> 00:18:42.140
<i>He's a chicken game survivor after all.</i>

00:18:42.140 --> 00:18:44.300
<i>Discarding this without wavering</i>

00:18:44.300 --> 00:18:47.940
<i>already places him in a realm 
common people will never reach.</i>

00:18:47.940 --> 00:18:50.810
<i>There is no hesitation at all.</i>

00:18:51.430 --> 00:18:53.040
<b>- Next Turn -</b>

00:18:52.140 --> 00:18:53.890
Akagi's next draw.

00:18:58.130 --> 00:19:02.420
<i>He made the 6 of characters pass, but holds 
on to the winning tile, the 7 of bamboo.</i>

00:19:02.420 --> 00:19:03.660
<i>A precise read...</i>

00:19:04.110 --> 00:19:08.000
<i>Along with a strong heart that allows 
him to utilize it under pressure.</i>

00:19:08.720 --> 00:19:13.260
<i>Akagi Shigeru's most 
outstanding talent is...</i>

00:19:13.550 --> 00:19:16.270
<i>the ability to trust his own judgment.</i>

00:19:16.270 --> 00:19:18.020
<i>His unwavering heart.</i>

00:19:20.570 --> 00:19:25.190
Yagi's plan to crack Akagi with 
pressure utterly fails.

00:19:25.780 --> 00:19:29.320
It seemed as if the game's flow 
was in Akagi's favor when...

00:19:36.710 --> 00:19:39.130
<i>No! That's Yagi's winning tile!</i>

00:19:39.910 --> 00:19:41.000
<i>Why?</i>

00:19:41.410 --> 00:19:43.130
<i>Why didn't you win with that tile?</i>

00:19:44.730 --> 00:19:46.860
<i>This is my plan.</i>

00:19:47.540 --> 00:19:50.870
<i>I don't need to win this trash hand.</i>

00:19:52.050 --> 00:19:56.820
<i>If I don't win, Akagi will 
probably win East Hand 1.</i>

00:19:57.370 --> 00:20:00.610
<i>But that's all right.</i>

00:20:01.250 --> 00:20:03.130
<i>Akagi will pick up some points,</i>

00:20:03.130 --> 00:20:05.380
<i>but along with them, 
he'll pick up some doubts.</i>

00:20:06.140 --> 00:20:09.640
<i>Those doubts will eventually hit 
him hard, like a body blow.</i>

00:20:09.990 --> 00:20:12.190
<i>Once he starts questioning 
his own judgment,</i>

00:20:12.190 --> 00:20:15.580
<i>he will seriously doubt himself towards 
the end, when the pressure's on.</i>

00:20:16.120 --> 00:20:19.420
<i>Then, he's likely to make a bad discard.</i>

00:20:19.930 --> 00:20:23.630
<i>That one mistake can be 
fatal in a match like this.</i>

00:20:24.060 --> 00:20:27.020
<i>I've survived in this 
world longer than you.</i>

00:20:27.020 --> 00:20:29.640
<i>The way I keep myself alive 
is different than yours.</i>

00:20:30.440 --> 00:20:34.880
<i>You're already half-caught in my trap.</i>

00:20:35.620 --> 00:20:37.280
But in the 15th turn...

00:20:39.610 --> 00:20:42.370
<i>There it is! Ryanpeikou, pinfu, tsumo!</i>

00:20:39.610 --> 00:20:42.370
Ryanpeikou - A double sequence, here 33-44-55 and 77-88-99. 
Pinfu - A hand of only sequences and a 2 or more sided wait.

00:20:42.370 --> 00:20:43.290
<i>Mangan!</i>

00:20:49.220 --> 00:20:50.970
Akagi does not take the win.

00:20:52.540 --> 00:20:55.110
<i>That bastard... could he...</i>

00:20:55.750 --> 00:20:57.970
The unwavering heart 
of the genius Akagi.

00:20:59.450 --> 00:21:01.510
The endless schemes of Yagi.

00:21:01.860 --> 00:21:04.320
The quiet duel to the 
death between two rivals...

00:21:05.060 --> 00:21:07.280
The night of legends continues...

00:22:16.210 --> 00:22:18.820
Next Episode: "The Sinister Scheme."

00:22:17.130 --> 00:22:20.550
<b>Next Episode</b>

00:22:17.130 --> 00:22:20.550
<b>The Sinister Scheme</b>
